  "review_findings_fixed": [
    "Creation authority is revalidated after weighted host admission before directory allocation/export, so expiry while waiting produces no backup directory",
    "Replication authority and selected manifest are revalidated inside the core backup lock after admission, archive preparation and before receipt/content network calls",
    "Pending selection pins exact source/endpoint/size/manifest and forbids reconfiguration; receipt must match the selected manifest before pending is cleared",
    "Selection leases use distinct occurrence identities; source selection/pinning serializes with retention and oldest-first ordering avoids starving older backups"
  ],
  "limits": [
    "One interval policy each for creation, replication and retention; no calendar expressions, remote service retention policy, credential manager or automatic authority renewal.",
    "Ordinary grant expiry applies; fresh explicit runtime or the required schedule/effect verb grants are needed. CLI defaults to one hour; intervals must fit remaining grant lifetime.",
    "Creation errors leaving allocated partial backups and interrupted creation claims block until investigation and explicit resume. No unproven partial backup is adopted or force-deleted.",
    "Replication missing or mismatched remote proof retains pending selection; no forced reupload, abandon or destination change while pending. A claim with no durable upload attempt may safely make its first transfer.",
    "Replication sources remain monotonically pinned. Retention can preserve more than keep; no unpin API or storage quota policy is added.",
    "Successful outbox records prevent repeated uploads but do not establish future remote availability; this is not continuous remote health monitoring.",
    "Native and real-provider network mutations use disposable localhost adapter/replica services. No vendor-specific remote deployment is supplied.",
    "Formal checks prove explicit production predicates and bounded lifecycle/order models, not full filesystem, Dolt, HTTP, Git or provider implementations."
  ],
